{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfUUM8nzfDtpiuZErS1mH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Quick math:** 25 referrals/month × $180 = $4,500/month = $54,000/year in captured revenue *if you capture all of it*. Net of the $9,000/year service cost, that's ~$45,000/year against a $110,000 purchase — roughly a 2.5-year payback purely from referrals you already generate internally, before touching the shared-arrangement volume.\n\nThe idle capacity you're worried about is actually the tell here: the machine's marginal cost per additional scan is near zero once bought, so any extra volume from the neighboring practice is close to pure upside with no added capex. That flips the framing — the shared arrangement isn't really an alternative to buying, it's what makes buying earn out faster and de-risks the bet that you'll actually capture most of those 25 referrals (some patients may still prefer the specialist regardless of convenience, insurance panel, or your current referral relationships).\n\nMy recommendation: **negotiate concrete volume/revenue terms with the neighboring practice first**, before committing the $110K. If they can credibly commit to enough shared volume to push utilization well above the ~17% (of full capacity) break-even on service costs, the purchase decision becomes easy. If they can't commit to real numbers (just a vague \"we'll send you cases\"), you're back to the standalone 25/month case — still plausible at ~2.5 years, but worth stress-testing your actual expected capture rate (not 100%) before signing anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16590,"cache_read_input_tokens":24048,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16590},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnos